<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Qilian Mountains</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Qilian Mountains are a range on the north-eastern edge of the Tibetan Plateau, along the border of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu">Gansu</a> and Qinghai in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. Their snowmelt waters the oases of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu-corridor">Hexi Corridor</a>, the route of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a> between China and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>. Chinese sources place the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuezhi">Yuezhi</a> between the Qilian Mountains and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dunhuang">Dunhuang</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/qilian-mountains.jpg" alt="Qilian Mountains" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Neil Young (cnneil (Flickr) · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Qilian_in_Qilian_Qinghai.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
